#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161852 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162052 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "233ee0d9-ce64-5d75-ac81-351ec0ac8f4b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transformations Where the Change-of-Variables Method Is Applicable"}] :question/id #uuid "14830c55-32bd-5eb9-adb6-b91675061a21" :question/math-academy-id "e-9100" :question/problem "Suppose that $X$ is a continuous random variable. For which of the following transformations can the change-of-variables method be applied to find the probability density function of $Y$?\n\n1. $Y = X^4$, where $X\\in (0,2)$\n2. $Y = \\cos X$, where $X\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$\n3. $Y = e^{-X}$, where $X\\in (0, \\infty)$" :question/worked-solution "The change-of-variables method can be applied when a transformation $Y = u(X)$ is strictly increasing or strictly decreasing (i.e., strictly monotonic) over the entire support of $X$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation I is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x) = x^4$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (0,2)$.\n- Transformation II is *not* strictly monotonic. For example, $u(x) = \\cos x$ is decreasing on $x \\in \\left(0,\\pi \\right)$ and increasing on $x \\in \\left(\\pi,2\\pi\\right)$.\n- Transformation III is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x) =e^{-x}$ is strictly decreasing for $x\\in (0,\\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161853 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162053 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26854b8e-68bc-5e69-8fcd-fa46b72d05c3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under an Affine Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "2fd76ea7-ab08-5afa-9762-158a85999321" :question/math-academy-id "e-9101" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n${\\frac{1}{156}x^{3},\\begin{vmatrix}1 < x < 5 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n\nIf $Y = \\dfrac{1-X}{4}$, use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y$." :question/worked-solution "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function $f_X$ and $Y = u(X)$, where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n${f_{X}(u^{-1}(y)) \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}(u^{-1})^{′}(y) &,\\end{vmatrix}yis betweenu(a)andu(b); 0, \\mid otherwise$.\n\nThe transformation\n\n$$\nu(x) = \\dfrac{1-x}{4}\n$$\n\nis strictly monotonically *decreasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find $u^{-1}(y)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(y) &= \\frac{1 - y}{4} \\\\\ny &= (1 - u^{-1}(y))/(4) \\\\\nu^{-1}(y) &= 1 - 4y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $(u^{-1})'(y){:}$\n\n$$\n(u^{-1})'(y) = -4\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero $f_Y$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(y) &= f_{X}(u^{-1}(y)) \\cdot \\mid (u^{-1})^{′}(y) \\mid \\\\\n&= f_{X}(1 - 4y) \\cdot \\mid - 4 \\mid \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{156}(1 - 4y)^{3} \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{39}(1 - 4y)^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $f_X$ is nonzero on $1 < x < 5$, the bounds are $a=1$ and $b=5$, we have\n\n$$\nu(1) = 0, \\quad u(5) = -1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{39}(1 - 4y)^{3},\\begin{vmatrix}-1 < y < 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161854 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162054 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90802916-605f-5a66-b05b-908388c01ee9" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under a Nonlinear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "fd8ceb2f-55e5-588f-ae79-015f58007fba" :question/math-academy-id "e-9102" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n${3x^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}0 < x < 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n\nIf $Y = \\sqrt{X}$, use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y$." :question/worked-solution "If $X \\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function $f_X$ and $Y = u(X)$, where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n${f_{X}(u^{-1}(y)) \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}(u^{-1})^{′}(y) &,\\end{vmatrix}yis betweenu(a)andu(b); 0, \\mid otherwise$.\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nY = \\sqrt{X}\n$$\n\nThe transformation\n\n$$\nu(x) = \\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nis strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find $u^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(y) &= \\sqrt{y} \\\\\ny &= \\sqrt{u^{-1}(y)} \\\\\nu^{-1}(y) &= y^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $(u^{-1})'(y){:}$\n\n$$\n(u^{-1})'(y) = 2y\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to the change-of-variable method, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(y) &= f_{X}(u^{-1}(y)) \\cdot \\mid (u^{-1})^{′}(y) \\mid \\\\\n&= f_{X}(y^{2}) \\cdot \\mid 2y \\mid \\\\\n&= 3(y^{2})^{2} \\cdot 2y \\\\\n&= 3y^{4} \\cdot 2y \\\\\n&= 6y^{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $f_X$ is nonzero on $0 < x < 1$, the bounds are $a = 0$ and $b = 1$, we have\n\n$$\nu(0) = 0, \\quad u(1) = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n${6y^{5},\\begin{vmatrix}0 < y < 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162054 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90802916-605f-5a66-b05b-908388c01ee9" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under a Nonlinear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbf07dc1-1fc9-5eae-a787-d9b6aa26bd39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f_{Y}(y) = {\\frac{3}{14}\\sqrt{\\ln y},\\begin{vmatrix}1 < y < 4 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158877} {:answer/id #uuid "0ea180f2-ff32-55ae-87a1-fa5cfc1b897f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f_{Y}(y) = {\\frac{3}{28}\\ln y,\\begin{vmatrix}0 < y < \\ln 4 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158878} {:answer/id #uuid "3ce9061c-9e73-5a11-aec5-6c9b323145a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f_{Y}(y) = {\\frac{3}{14}e^{3y/2},\\begin{vmatrix}0 < y < \\ln 4 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158879} {:answer/id #uuid "55b7a4aa-0e15-50f3-b216-9451686351ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f_{Y}(y) = {\\frac{3}{28}\\ln y,\\begin{vmatrix}1 < y < 4 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158880} {:answer/id #uuid "74d979c3-ff7a-55c7-b4cf-271c28ee0673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f_{Y}(y) = {\\frac{3}{14}e^{y},\\begin{vmatrix}e < y < e^{4} \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158881}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6a88399b-1bc1-50e9-b55f-1a6bb18a1b16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158876}] :question/id #uuid "1fc46307-e5a1-551b-b8cf-8fa5407926f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-109183" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n$f_{X}(x) = {\\frac{3}{14}\\sqrt{x},\\begin{vmatrix}1 < x < 4 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n\nIf $Y = \\ln X$, use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162406 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162054 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90802916-605f-5a66-b05b-908388c01ee9" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under a Nonlinear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bfb13ba-b741-547e-a520-ac369dff746b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f_{Y}(y) = {\\frac{1}{e - 1},\\begin{vmatrix}1 < y < e \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159639} {:answer/id #uuid "09720734-fc26-5388-a71e-50ae677defe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f_{Y}(y) = {ye^{y - 2},\\begin{vmatrix}1 < y < 2 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159640} {:answer/id #uuid "1b8cbcfd-afa0-5082-8393-afd85356ca35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f_{Y}(y) = {2\\ln y,\\begin{vmatrix}1 < y < e \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159641} {:answer/id #uuid "3d7bc40f-2365-57fb-8af7-5362c04056f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f_{Y}(y) = {- (y\\ln (y))/(2),\\begin{vmatrix}e^{-1} < y < 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159642} {:answer/id #uuid "64f42f8f-b867-5753-b1c0-d5680a2a1840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f_{Y}(y) = {- (2\\ln (y))/(y),\\begin{vmatrix}e^{-1} < y < 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159643}] :answer-field/id #uuid "02e0b4df-b03a-5331-8fad-a3680e2a7c52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159638}] :question/id #uuid "9f7b4c3d-9f53-5a0a-9f65-57f007102f92" :question/math-academy-id "q-143899" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n$f_{X}(x) = {\\frac{2\\ln x}{x},\\begin{vmatrix}1 < x < e \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n\nIf $Y = \\frac{1}{X}$, use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y$."}]}
